      ],
      "source": "https://www.trawellino.com/destinations/united-states/kentucky_doe-valley",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Doe Valley began as a planned residential community launched in 1965 by local developers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doe_Valley%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Doe Valley centers on Doe Valley Lake, a reservoir on Doe Run, a tributary of the Ohio River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doe_Valley%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Land acquisition started in 1958 on 2,769 acres, and the dam and lake were finished by 1962.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/doe_valley_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the late 1700s, Kentucky was still a Virginia district, and early land grants covered the area that is now Doe Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/doe_valley_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Doe Run Creek powered early mills that drove the region’s economic growth.",
